      "claim": "GSK2838232 showed poor oral bioavailability in initial clinical studies.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Emixustat showed low oral bioavailability in healthy subjects, with plasma concentrations far below total drug-related exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C32"],
      "claim": "Curcumin has low oral bioavailability after oral administration in humans.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C19"],
      "claim": "Prochlorperazine mean oral bioavailability was 12.5%, below the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "DHPG (ganciclovir) oral bioavailability was 5.4% in humans, below the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is lumefantrine, a lipophilic dibutylamino alcohol with a chlorinated polycyclic core. The closest analogue halofantrine and multiple high-transfer drugs with similar physicochemical features consistently show low oral bioavailability (2-15%), below the 20% threshold. The single ~30% halofantrine estimate is an outlier based on indirect comparison and does not outweigh the consistent low-F evidence. Therefore predict low.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for lumefantrine in humans; prediction relies on close analogues.",
    "Halofantrine fasted F estimate (~30%) conflicts with other low-F reports, adding uncertainty.",
    "Formulation and food effects can markedly alter absorption of lipophilic drugs; the query's F may be higher with lipid-rich food, but threshold-relevant human data are lacking."
  ],
